{"subjectId":"python","id":"string-formatting","title":"String Formatting & f-strings","group":"Strings & Numbers","order":11,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">f-strings are the modern way to build text: any expression inside <code>{}</code>, plus a compact <em>format spec</em> after <code>:</code> that controls width, alignment, precision, signs and number bases.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-braces\"></i>f-string basics</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>name, items, price = \"Asha\", 3, 49.5\nprint(f\"{name} bought {items} items\")            # Asha bought 3 items\nprint(f\"total = {items * price}\")                # total = 148.5\nprint(f\"{name.upper()} {len(name)} {'yes' if items &gt; 2 else 'no'}\")   # ASHA 4 yes\nprint(f\"{{literal braces}} {items}\")             # {literal braces} 3\ndata = {\"k\": [10, 20]}\nprint(f\"{data['k'][1]}\")                         # 20</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Syntax</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Expression</td><td><code>{expr}</code></td><td><code>f'{a + b}'</code></td></tr><tr><td>Conversion</td><td><code>{expr!r}</code> / <code>!s</code> / <code>!a</code></td><td><code>repr()</code>, <code>str()</code>, <code>ascii()</code> of the value</td></tr><tr><td>Format spec</td><td><code>{expr:spec}</code></td><td><code>f'{pi:.3f}'</code></td></tr><tr><td>Self-documenting</td><td><code>{expr=}</code></td><td><code>f'{x=}'</code> → <code>x=5</code> (3.8+)</td></tr><tr><td>Nested spec</td><td><code>{expr:{width}.{prec}f}</code></td><td>Width/precision from variables</td></tr><tr><td>Literal brace</td><td><code>{{</code> and <code>}}</code></td><td><code>f'{{x}}'</code> → <code>{x}</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bug\"></i>Debugging with <code>=</code> and conversions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>x, word = 42, \"café\"\nprint(f\"{x=}\")                  # x=42\nprint(f\"{x * 2 = }\")            # x * 2 = 84\nprint(f\"{word=}\")               # word='café'   ← = uses repr() by default\nprint(f\"{word!s}\", f\"{word!r}\", f\"{word!a}\")   # café 'café' 'caf\\xe9'\nprint(f\"{x = :&gt;6}|\")            # x =     42|</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Tip</div><p><code>{x=}</code> prints the expression text, then the value's <code>repr()</code> — the quickest print-debugging tool in Python.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>The format-spec mini-language</h3>\n<div class=\"formula\"><code>[[fill]align][sign][z][#][0][width][grouping][.precision][type]</code></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Options</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>fill</td><td>any character</td><td>Pad character (needs an align after it)</td></tr><tr><td>align</td><td><code>&lt;</code> <code>&gt;</code> <code>^</code> <code>=</code></td><td>Left / right / centre / pad after the sign</td></tr><tr><td>sign</td><td><code>+</code> <code>-</code> space</td><td>Always show sign / only negatives (default) / space for positives</td></tr><tr><td><code>#</code></td><td></td><td>Alternate form: <code>0b</code>, <code>0o</code>, <code>0x</code> prefixes</td></tr><tr><td><code>0</code></td><td></td><td>Zero-pad numbers (sign-aware)</td></tr><tr><td>width</td><td>integer</td><td>Minimum field width</td></tr><tr><td>grouping</td><td><code>,</code> or <code>_</code></td><td>Thousands separator</td></tr><tr><td>.precision</td><td>integer</td><td>Digits after the point (<code>f</code>, <code>%</code>), significant digits (<code>g</code>), or max chars for strings</td></tr><tr><td>type</td><td><code>d b o x X e f g % n s c</code></td><td>Presentation: decimal, binary, octal, hex, exponent, fixed, general, percent…</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-align-left\"></i>Alignment &amp; padding</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>s = \"py\"\nprint(f\"[{s:&lt;6}] [{s:&gt;6}] [{s:^6}]\")      # [py    ] [    py] [  py  ]\nprint(f\"[{s:*^8}] [{s:-&gt;5}]\")             # [***py***] [---py]\nprint(f\"[{42:5}] [{'ab':5}]\")             # [   42] [ab   ]   ← numbers right, strings left by default\nprint(f\"[{-42:=+7}] [{-42:07}]\")          # [-    42] [-000042]\nprint(f\"[{'truncate me':.5}]\")            # [trunc]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-number\"></i>Numbers: precision, signs, grouping</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>pi, big, ratio = 3.14159265, 1234567.891, 0.4567\nprint(f\"{pi:.2f} {pi:.4f} {pi:8.3f}|\")    # 3.14 3.1416    3.142|\nprint(f\"{big:,.2f} {big:_.1f}\")           # 1,234,567.89 1_234_567.9\nprint(f\"{ratio:.1%} {ratio:%}\")           # 45.7% 45.670000%\nprint(f\"{big:e} {big:.2e}\")               # 1.234568e+06 1.23e+06\nprint(f\"{pi:g} {big:g} {0.00001234:g}\")   # 3.14159 1.23457e+06 1.234e-05\nprint(f\"{5:+d} {-5:+d} {5: d}\")           # +5 -5  5\nprint(f\"{1_000_000:,}\")                   # 1,000,000</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary\"></i>Integers in other bases</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>n = 255\nprint(f\"{n:b} {n:o} {n:x} {n:X}\")         # 11111111 377 ff FF\nprint(f\"{n:#b} {n:#o} {n:#x}\")            # 0b11111111 0o377 0xff\nprint(f\"{5:08b}\")                         # 00000101\nprint(f\"{n:#06x}\")                        # 0x00ff   ← width counts the prefix\nprint(f\"{65:c}\")                          # A\nprint(format(n, \"x\"), int(\"ff\", 16))      # ff 255</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack\"></i>Nested &amp; dynamic specs</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>rows = [(\"apple\", 1.5), (\"kiwi\", 12.25)]\nw = max(len(name) for name, _ in rows)\nfor name, cost in rows:\n    print(f\"{name:&lt;{w}} | {cost:&gt;{8}.{2}f}\")</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>apple |     1.50\nkiwi  |    12.25</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calendar\"></i>Dates in f-strings</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from datetime import datetime\nd = datetime(2026, 9, 29, 14, 5)\nprint(f\"{d:%Y-%m-%d %H:%M}\")        # 2026-09-29 14:05\nprint(f\"{d:%d %b %Y, %A}\")          # 29 Sep 2026, Tuesday\nprint(f\"{d:%I:%M %p}\")              # 02:05 PM</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>A format spec is passed to the object's <code>__format__</code> method — that is why <code>datetime</code> understands <code>strftime</code> codes after the colon.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-wand\"></i><code>__format__</code> for your own classes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Money:\n    def __init__(self, amount):\n        self.amount = amount\n    def __format__(self, spec):\n        if spec == \"inr\":\n            return f\"₹{self.amount:,.2f}\"\n        return format(self.amount, spec)     # fall back to float formatting\n\nm = Money(1234.5)\nprint(f\"{m:inr}\")              # ₹1,234.50\nprint(f\"{m:&gt;10.1f}|\")          #     1234.5|\nprint(format(m, \"inr\"))        # ₹1,234.50</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-history\"></i>Older styles: <code>str.format</code>, <code>%</code> and <code>Template</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(\"{} + {} = {}\".format(1, 2, 3))              # 1 + 2 = 3\nprint(\"{0}{1}{0}\".format(\"ab\", \"-\"))               # ab-ab\nprint(\"{name} is {age}\".format(name=\"Ravi\", age=30))   # Ravi is 30\npoint = {\"x\": 3, \"y\": 4}\nprint(\"({p[x]}, {p[y]})\".format(p=point))          # (3, 4)\nprint(\"{:&gt;6.2f}|\".format(3.14159))                 #   3.14|\n\nprint(\"%s has %d items (%.1f%%)\" % (\"cart\", 3, 42.456))   # cart has 3 items (42.5%)\nprint(\"%(who)s says hi\" % {\"who\": \"Asha\"})         # Asha says hi\n\nfrom string import Template\nt = Template(\"Hello $name, you owe $$${amount}\")\nprint(t.substitute(name=\"Kiran\", amount=20))       # Hello Kiran, you owe $20\nprint(t.safe_substitute(name=\"Kiran\"))             # Hello Kiran, you owe $${amount}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>f-strings</td><td><strong>Default choice</strong> — fastest and most readable (3.6+)</td></tr><tr><td><code>str.format</code></td><td>Templates stored separately from the values, e.g. <code>FMT.format(**row)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%</code> formatting</td><td>Legacy code and <code>logging</code> calls (<code>log.info('%s', x)</code> formats lazily)</td></tr><tr><td><code>string.Template</code></td><td>Templates written by <strong>users</strong> — <code>$name</code> only, no expressions, so it's safe</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Security</div><p>Never call <code>.format()</code> or build f-strings from untrusted template text — <code>'{0.__class__}'.format(obj)</code> can read attributes. Use <code>string.Template</code> for user-supplied templates.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sparkles\"></i>Python 3.12+: f-strings grew up</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>songs = [\"a\", \"b\"]\nprint(f\"{\", \".join(songs)}\")          # a, b     ← same quotes inside (3.12+)\nprint(f\"{'\\n'.join(songs)!r}\")        # 'a\\nb'   ← backslashes allowed in expressions\ntotal = f\"{\n    sum([1, 2, 3])                   # comments and line breaks inside {} are fine\n}\"\nprint(total)                          # 6</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Version</div><p>PEP 701 (3.12) made f-strings part of the real grammar: reuse the outer quote type, write backslashes and comments inside the braces, and nest arbitrarily. Python 3.14 adds <strong>t-strings</strong> (<code>t'…'</code>) that produce a <code>Template</code> object for safe, deferred processing (e.g. SQL/HTML escaping).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Pitfalls</h3>\n<ul><li><code>f'{x:.2f}'</code> on a string raises <code>ValueError: Unknown format code 'f'</code> — convert first.</li><li><code>round(x, 2)</code> changes the value; <code>f'{x:.2f}'</code> only changes how it is <em>displayed</em>. For money use <code>Decimal</code>.</li><li><code>=</code> in <code>{x=}</code> uses <code>repr</code>, so strings appear quoted — add <code>!s</code> for the plain form.</li><li>Forgetting the <code>f</code> prefix silently prints the braces: <code>'{name}'</code>.</li></ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you print a float with 2 decimals and thousands separators, right-aligned in 12 characters?</summary><div><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(f\"{1234567.891:&gt;12,.2f}|\")   # 1,234,567.89|</code></pre></figure><p>Spec parts in order: align <code>&gt;</code>, width <code>12</code>, grouping <code>,</code>, precision <code>.2</code>, type <code>f</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Which string formatting method is fastest?</summary><div><p>f-strings — they compile to direct bytecode (<code>FORMAT_VALUE</code> / <code>BUILD_STRING</code>) with no method lookup or argument parsing, so they beat <code>str.format</code> and <code>%</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}